Chapter: T3 Narrow Trap

 A bounds check nothing has ever seen fire is not a bounds check. This
 program narrows one value that is inside its band and one that is outside,
 and the second must stop the machine. `scale` exists so the static bounds
 prover cannot see the value: a literal out of range is a compile error
 (CDX2050) and would never reach the emitted check at all.

 Expected under the emulator: the first two lines print, then the literal
 prefix of the third, then `TRAP: division by zero`, which is what the
 emitted check deliberately provokes. The prefix appears here and not on
 x86-64 because a print is linearised into one syscall per leaf on this
 target, so the literal is out before the value that traps is computed.

 Division by zero is not the only fault this machine raises -- under spec
 v1.3 an unknown syscall, a heap allocation past the top of memory and a
 malformed instruction all trap too, and every trap halts with exit 70. It
 is simply the one an emitted check can provoke deliberately from a
 register it already holds.

Section: Body

  takes-small : Integer between 0 and 100 -> Integer between 0 and 100
  takes-small (b) = b

  scale : Integer between 0 and 100 -> Integer between 0 and 100000
  scale (n) = n * 50

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("in band: " & show (takes-small (__narrow (scale 1))))
    print-line-uni ("about to narrow 2000 into 0..100")
    print-line-uni ("must not print: " & show (takes-small (__narrow (scale 40))))
  end
